# Git Workflow

```text
feature/*  →  PR  →  staging  →  CI / review  →  PR  →  main  →  tag v* / production
```

- **Never push directly to `main`.** Production changes land only through a
  pull request. The required CI job name is **`quality`** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`).
- **Default integration branch:** `staging`. Day-to-day work and feature PRs
  target `staging`. CI runs on `staging` and `main`.
- **`main`:** production. Prefer a squash-merge from `staging` (or a focused
  release branch) so `main` stays one commit per release.
- After `main` updates, `sync-staging.yml` **merge-commits** `main` back into
  `staging` (or opens a PR if it conflicts). That is what keeps squash from
  stacking version/`CHANGELOG` conflicts on the next release. The sync PR
  itself must not be squashed.
- Purpose-based short-lived branches are fine; merge into `staging` first when possible.
- Commit only when asked. Messages explain why.
- Never commit secrets.
- Small, reviewable diffs. Include summary, test plan, and docs when architecture or business rules change.
- Product releases: bump versions on `staging`, open PR → `main`, then tag `v*` (on the merged main commit preferred) to run Product Release.
- GitHub branch protection / rulesets need a public repo or GitHub Pro on a
  private repo. Until that is enabled, treat the policy above as mandatory
  and do not use `git push origin main`.
